From 823ed5e6ffd627f81d07b2f7527f06678744fcd2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: havok2-htwo <havok2-htwo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 10:34:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] conversation cache: parked K/V segments and checkpoint
 state can be mapped one by one, so an eviction returns its RAM to the kernel
 (opt-in STRATA_CONV_MMAP=1, #3115)

Parked conversations (ConversationBuffer segments, ConversationCheckpoint gdn / ple / tails / dead / block_pos)
come from malloc.  glibc serves a request from a free arena chunk before it consults the mmap threshold, raises a
dynamic threshold above 16 MiB after the first mmapped free, and shrinks a thread arena only at its top heap's top.
So what an eviction frees stays resident in the arenas; the physical-RAM gate's next MemAvailable / cgroup reading
does not move, and the gate can evict the whole cache and still refuse.  Production host: 1,946 arena heaps with
108 GiB RSS; an evict-all of 22 GiB left "available" unchanged; 15 GB of new snapshots admitted with no RSS growth.

STRATA_CONV_MMAP=1 (read once, at the first allocation): include/strata/core/conversation_alloc.hpp's
ConversationAllocator maps every allocation of at least 64 KiB on its own (page-rounded anonymous mmap) and
munmaps it on free; smaller ones and the switch unset go through operator new exactly as std::allocator does.
- ConversationBuffer::Segment uses it with no value-initialization: resize() still passes its fill value, except for
  a fresh mapping filled with 0, which is zero already (no second pass over the pages).  A mapped segment's capacity
  is its whole pages, so bytes() counts what is resident and the last page's spare bytes serve the next append.
- ConversationCheckpoint's running-state vectors are ConversationBytes (std::vector<uint8_t> with the allocator,
  value-initializing as before).  Copy / move (O(1), stateless allocator) / operator== are std::vector's.
- Plain pageable memory either way; nothing calls cudaHostRegister on it.
- The two comments that said an eviction is back with the kernel (generate.cpp's RAM gate,
  ConversationCache::evict_oldest) now describe the arena retention and the switch; serve logs a line when it is on.
- conversation_alloc_test (CPU only, with and without the switch): container semantics (zeroing over stale bytes,
  fill values, growth, copy / move / ==) and RssAnon falling back to its baseline after parking ~2.2 GiB from four
  threads and evicting it (without the switch, 427 MiB stayed resident in the same run).

Without the variable the engine allocates as before (the checkpoint vectors' type changed; their behaviour did not).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
